<span class="eyebrow">Imber</span>
<h1>Imber: Tools it can use</h1>

<p class="doc-lead">
  Imber acts only through a fixed set of tools, grouped in families; each family can be switched off in Settings, and a family that is off is not even offered to the model.
</p>

<h2>The families</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Family (Settings label)</th><th>Tools</th><th>What it is for</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Read and search files</strong></td><td><code>search_text</code>, <code>find_files</code>, <code>structural_search</code>, <code>bennu_read_file</code>, <code>read_files</code>, <code>file_outline</code></td><td>Read a file (or several in one call), see a file's declarations with their line ranges, search text, find files by name, and find or count code by its shape (<code>structural_search</code>: the same query language as the Structural search window — holes for statements, members, parameters and modifiers, <code>or</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>as</code>, <code>inside</code>, <code>group</code> — answering with counts, a sample of places or the <code>group</code> table; it never writes).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Project intelligence</strong></td><td><code>bennu_find_symbol</code>, <code>bennu_find_usages</code>, <code>bennu_references</code>, <code>bennu_callers</code>, <code>bennu_symbol_at</code>, <code>bennu_outline</code>, <code>bennu_check_file</code>, <code>bennu_problems</code>, <code>bennu_catalog</code>, <code>bennu_frameworks</code>, <code>bennu_module_graph</code>, <code>bennu_project_summary</code>, <code>bennu_tests</code>, <code>bennu_todos</code> and more</td><td>Bennu's own knowledge of the project: symbols, usages, beans, endpoints, framework catalogues, problems, tests, TODOs, the module graph, the profiler summary. A question about a position (symbol, declaration, references, implementations, rename, problems) is answered by the one engine that serves the file — the editor's own: a language server for its languages, the Java index, the Patina package for <code>.ptn</code>, the shader engine for <code>.wgsl</code> without <code>wgsl-analyzer</code>. When nothing serves a file the answer says why (no server for the extension, the server not installed, no project of its kind above the file) instead of reporting nothing found. While a language server is still loading, its answers say so, and <code>bennu_wait_ready</code> (given a file of that language) starts the server if nobody has and waits until it can answer — 60 seconds by default, at most 300 — instead of asking again and again. Engines do not all answer the same questions — a Lua server has no implementations or callers, the shader engine no rename, a Cargo manifest only completion and problems — so a tool asked something its engine does not answer refuses it, saying what that engine does answer; and <code>bennu_project_summary</code> lists, per language of the project, which questions its engine answers (a language server's own list once it is running).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Framework model</strong></td><td><code>bennu_endpoints</code>, <code>bennu_beans</code>, <code>bennu_entities</code>, <code>bennu_scheduled_jobs</code>, <code>bennu_config_properties</code>, <code>bennu_dependency_conflicts</code></td><td>Typed, bounded lists built from the framework models: HTTP endpoints (Spring MVC / WebFlux, JAX-RS, servlets, Struts: method, path, handler, parameters, produces / consumes), DI beans (scope, profile, conditions, injection points and who injects them), JPA entities with relations and repositories, <code>@Scheduled</code> jobs, configuration keys and who reads them, and dependency version conflicts. Filter with <code>name</code> / <code>path</code> / <code>type</code> and a <code>limit</code>; a cut list says how many rows were left out. <code>bennu_dependency_conflicts</code> runs the build tool and is in the <em>Run</em> family.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Edit files</strong></td><td><code>edit_file</code>, <code>edit_files</code>, <code>replace_in_files</code>, <code>create_file</code>, <code>delete_file</code></td><td>Exact edits (one or a list across files, all or nothing), text or regular-expression replace over a folder with a cap on files and a dry run that shows every match with its line and what it would become, create, delete. Arguments that arrive broken are not run: Imber is told where they went wrong and sends them again.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Refactoring</strong></td><td><code>rename_symbol</code>, <code>add_parameter</code></td><td>Rename a symbol or add a parameter across every file that mentions it, from the project index, as one change. A renamed public type's file is renamed with it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Structural search and replace</strong></td><td><code>structural_replace</code></td><td>Rewrite code by its shape, safer than a regular expression. (Searching by shape is <code>structural_search</code>, in the read family.) Both walk only the files Imber may reach: a folder outside its scope that you did not open for it, a secret file (<code>.env</code>, keys, certificates) and <code>.git</code> are skipped before they are read — they are in no count, table or sample — and for a rewrite so is anything it could not also change (a folder opened for reading only, or everything while editing is off). The answer says how many files were skipped, never which.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Local History</strong></td><td><code>local_history</code></td><td>List a file's revisions, show a diff, list deleted files, restore one.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Build, run and test</strong></td><td><code>bennu_check</code>, <code>bennu_build</code>, <code>bennu_test_run</code>, <code>bennu_cargo_run</code>, <code>bennu_npm_run_script</code>, <code>run_tool</code>, <code>run_script</code>, run configurations, launching a program</td><td>Anything that executes the project, and the programs and scripts you let it run. <code>bennu_check</code> says whether the project compiles: <code>cargo check</code> on Cargo (with a <code>target</code>, for that triple: "does it compile for wasm?"), <code>mvn compile</code> on Maven. <code>bennu_build</code> (Cargo) builds the files themselves — a real <code>cargo build</code> of the library and binaries for the host or a <code>target</code> (<code>wasm32-wasip2</code>…), with a <code>profile</code> (<code>release</code>…) — and its answer lists the artifacts it produced with their paths: the <code>.wasm</code> to install, for example, which a program you set up can then copy. A conversation that builds in its own target directory is told the artifacts are there, not in <code>target/</code>. <code>run_tool</code> runs the programs you set up in Settings › Imber (see Imber › Running and observing). On Cargo, <code>bennu_test_run</code> takes the features to enable (<code>features</code>, <code>all_features</code>, <code>no_default_features</code>), a module prefix (<code>cli::</code>) or a crate's package name as well as test names, and the runner — <code>cargo</code> or <code>nextest</code>; left out, nextest when the workspace has a <code>.config/nextest.toml</code>. With no <code>root</code>, a <code>module</code> that only another project of the conversation has runs in that project (also for <code>bennu_check</code> and <code>bennu_build</code>); otherwise name the project as <code>root</code>. A module prefix (<code>cli::</code>) runs every test under it, in each crate that has that module; a name not found is looked for again after a fresh scan, and the error lists the modules that hold tests, by crate and binary. <code>ignored_only</code> runs only the <code>#[ignore]</code>d tests of the selection, and <code>filter</code> takes a nextest filter expression (<code>-E</code>) for a selection names cannot say. When no test ran, the answer carries the compiler's (or Maven's) errors. While the project does not compile, <code>bennu_check</code> lists only the errors and counts the warnings; with no error, warnings from crates outside the project are counted, not listed. See Imber › Permissions and modes for when each asks.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Music (Merula)</strong></td><td><code>merula_check</code>, <code>merula_render</code></td><td>For <code>.merula</code> files, through Merula itself. <code>merula_check</code> (in the <em>Project</em> family) evaluates a file as Merula's editor does, without playing it, and lists every error and warning with its line and column — syntax, evaluation, instruments or samples the sound registry does not know, speech controls on a non-speech source — with the number of tracks and the length it renders. <code>merula_render</code> (in the <em>Run</em> family) checks the file, then renders it to OGG (or WAV) next to it or at <code>out</code>, and answers with the file, its length and size, or with the errors when there are any. The length is the file's <code>meta { cycles = "N" }</code>, else its arrangement's, else one cycle, unless <code>cycles</code> says otherwise. A render asks as the project's build does, by the mode, and shows in Merula's Downloads &amp; Exports with its Stop; stopping the run stops it too. Imber cannot hear the result: you do.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Live services</strong></td><td><code>run_start</code>, <code>cargo_run</code>, <code>run_status</code>, <code>run_logs</code>, <code>run_stop</code>, <code>wait_ready</code>, <code>list_recipes</code>, <code>run_recipe</code>, <code>http_request</code>, <code>trace_request</code>, <code>timeline</code>, <code>list_scenarios</code>, <code>run_scenario</code>, <code>save_scenario</code>, <code>console_logs</code>, <code>tail_file</code>, <code>wait_log</code>, <code>log_facets</code>, <code>log_record</code></td><td>Start the project's services (a Rust binary or example with its features and arguments through <code>cargo_run</code>), read their logs, call them over localhost, follow one request through every log, run scenarios. See Imber › Running and observing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Debugger</strong></td><td><code>debug_set_breakpoint</code>, <code>debug_list_breakpoints</code>, <code>debug_remove_breakpoint</code>, <code>debug_launch</code>, <code>debug_attach</code>, <code>debug_wait_stop</code>, <code>debug_stack</code>, <code>debug_variables</code>, <code>debug_evaluate</code>, <code>debug_step</code>, <code>debug_resume</code>, <code>debug_detach</code>, <code>debug_output</code>, <code>debug_run_to</code>, <code>debug_break_on_exception</code>, <code>debug_select_thread</code></td><td>Debug a Java program. See Imber › Debugging with Imber.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Look into a running Bevy game</strong></td><td><code>brp_connect</code>, <code>brp_query</code>, <code>brp_get</code>, <code>brp_resources</code>, <code>brp_get_resource</code>, <code>brp_types</code>, <code>brp_watch</code>, <code>brp_screenshot</code>, <code>brp_stats</code>, <code>brp_actions</code>, <code>brp_states</code></td><td>Read entities, components, resources and types over the Bevy Remote Protocol. Only for projects with a <code>Cargo.toml</code>. See Imber › Bevy games and the profiler.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Screenshots of a page</strong></td><td><code>screenshot_page</code>, <code>page_diagnostics</code></td><td>Look at a front end on localhost. See Imber › Screenshots and diagrams.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Switches that are not in the family list</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Let it change a running Bevy game</strong> (<code>brp_mutate</code>, <code>brp_insert</code>, <code>brp_remove</code>, <code>brp_spawn</code>, <code>brp_despawn</code>, <code>brp_set_resource</code>, <code>brp_reparent</code>, <code>brp_call</code>, <code>brp_key</code>, <code>brp_type</code>, <code>brp_mouse</code>, <code>brp_gamepad</code>, <code>brp_action</code>, <code>brp_set_state</code>): off by default, needs the Bevy family on.</li>
  <li><strong>Let it change the program it debugs</strong> (<code>debug_throw</code>, <code>debug_force_return</code>, <code>debug_drop_frame</code>, <code>debug_set_value</code>): off by default, needs the Debugger family on.</li>
  <li><strong>Git</strong> (<code>corvus_status</code>, <code>corvus_diff</code>, <code>corvus_log</code>, <code>corvus_blame</code>, <code>corvus_stage</code>, <code>corvus_unstage</code>, <code>corvus_commit</code>): off by default. See below.</li>
  <li><strong>Sub-agents</strong> (<code>spawn_agent</code>, <code>resume_agent</code>, <code>await_agents</code>): off by default; and the peer tools <code>file_owner</code> and <code>request_change</code> for talking to other conversations about a file. See Imber › Sub-agents.</li>
  <li><strong>Step plan</strong> (<code>update_plan</code>): on by default.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tools that are always there</h2>
<p>
  <code>ask_user</code> (questions, Imber › Decisions and questions), <code>bennu_help</code> (the help about Bennu itself), <code>open_ui</code> (opens a panel or window), <code>show_diagram</code> (draws a Mermaid diagram), <code>show_image</code> (shows a picture or an SVG, Imber › Screenshots and diagrams), <code>view_image</code> (Imber looks at an image file itself — a PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP a program, script or test wrote — received like a screenshot, up to 3.5 MB; only the latest two pictures stay in what it reads), <code>load_skill</code> (only when you enabled a skill, Imber › Skills) and <code>expand_result</code> (returns the detail of a digest). In a chat only <code>bennu_help</code>, <code>open_ui</code>, <code>show_diagram</code> and <code>show_image</code> are offered.
</p>

<h2>Switching a family off</h2>
<p>
  Settings › Imber › Tools it may use has one switch per family. A family that is off is not offered at all: Imber cannot call it, its definitions cost nothing, and a call that slips through is refused with "the user has switched that kind of tool off". "Do not touch my files" is <em>Edit files</em> off; "do not run anything" is <em>Build, run and test</em> and <em>Live services</em> off. The switches are the defaults for new conversations and for the next message of the open one.
</p>

<h2>Help about Bennu</h2>
<p>
  Ask how to do something in Bennu (a feature, a shortcut, where a panel is) and Imber looks it up in Bennu's own documentation, this manual, and in a map of the window's buttons and shortcuts, instead of answering from memory. If the documentation has nothing, it says so. It works in a chat too, and so does opening a panel: ask to see the dependencies, the problems or the tests and Imber opens them; in a chat that needs no access to your project.
</p>

<h2>Git</h2>
<p>
  Off by default; Settings › Imber › Git turns it on. Imber can then read the repository (status, diff, log, blame) and stage, unstage and commit through Corvus's own git tools. A commit always asks first, in every mode, and it cannot push. Git is not available in a chat.
</p>
<p>
  Reading means commit messages and author names go to the provider. <strong>Hide authors</strong> (on by default) shows the model each person as a made-up id such as <code>Author-7f3a9c</code>: the same person is always the same id, so "who wrote most of this" still works. The ids are turned back into names in what you read and in anything Imber sends to git, so a commit message crediting an id credits the real person. The mapping stays on this machine. Git also enables the "Git worktree" kind of isolated copy.
</p>

<h2>Several projects</h2>
<p>
  With the reach set to <em>All open projects</em> Imber works across the projects open in the workspace, the one you are in first, up to <em>Projects at once</em> (6 by default; each is a line in its instructions, so a higher number costs a little on every request). It reads, searches and changes in all of them: a tool takes a project by name (or <code>name:path</code>), the text search and the file finder take <code>project: "all"</code>, and a change goes to the project that owns the file, which keeps its own Local History. A proposal names a file in another project as <code>name:path</code>.
</p>

<h3>Bringing in another project</h3>
<p>
  Bennu's tools answer only for the projects of the conversation. When Imber needs them on another one, it names that project's root — usually with <code>bennu_project_summary</code>, which also says how far the index is — and the project is brought into the conversation before the call runs. Its instructions list the other projects you have open in Bennu, so it knows which are there. What happens depends on two things:
</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>The project</th><th>Imber may already read there</th><th>Imber may not read there</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Open in Bennu</strong> (already indexed)</td><td>It joins at once, without a question</td><td>The access card asks; a yes lets it join</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Not open in Bennu</strong> (any folder elsewhere)</td><td>As <em>Opening other projects</em> says (Settings › Imber › Access): ask, except in Autonomous (the default); always ask; open without asking; never</td><td>The access card asks, and says that a yes also opens and indexes it — the only question</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<ul>
  <li>An opened project is indexed in the background, as an AI client's would be: until the index is ready, Bennu answers "not yet" rather than "nothing". It does not appear among your tabs, and it is closed after the idle time like any project nobody is looking at.</li>
  <li>A project brought in stays part of the conversation for its later runs (while Arbor is open) and counts toward <em>Projects at once</em>; past that limit it is refused with the reason.</li>
  <li>The thread says when a project joined or was opened.</li>
  <li>Any language Bennu has a language server for works: opening a project starts every server whose root marker is at its root (<code>Cargo.toml</code>, <code>package.json</code>, <code>.luarc.json</code>, <code>go.mod</code>, <code>pyproject.toml</code>, <code>angular.json</code>, <code>svelte.config.js</code>, a server of your own…), and Imber's symbol, outline, references, callers, implementors, rename and problems tools answer from it. The framework model (endpoints, beans, entities) is Java's. See Language servers.</li>
  <li>Any folder can be brought in, but only what can be indexed is: a Maven or Gradle build gets the Java index, and each language server whose root marker is there is started. A folder with neither joins as files.</li>
  <li>Never the root of a disk or your home folder itself. Never from a scheduled run, a sub-agent (it works in its parent's projects) or an isolated copy (it sees only the copy). With <em>Never</em>, a project not open in Bennu is only files to Imber.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Big answers are digests</h2>
<p>
  A build with sixty errors, a test run with forty failures, a long search or a long list of usages is answered with a digest (counts, distinct problems grouped, the files most affected and what was left out); <code>expand_result</code> returns the detail for one file, test or message. A file Imber already read comes back as one line when unchanged, and as the lines that differ when it changed.
</p>
